  "modified": "2026-08-14T13:47:51Z",
  "summary": "nolimit-agent ships heavily obfuscated SMS/SMTP/SOCKS abuse toolkit under hidden.ad/ directory",
  "details": "The package ships 46 heavily obfuscated JavaScript files under a hidden `.ad/` directory, using javascript-obfuscator-style hex identifier mangling (`_0x56a72c`, `_0xf4f8a0`, etc.) across every file. File names disclose the operational purpose of the toolkit: `sms-providers.js`, `phone-validator.js`, `smtp-health-cache.js`, `socks-loader.js`, `web-command.js`, `from-generator.js`, and 40 additional single-letter/short-name modules (`x0.js` through `xz.js`). The naming — SMS provider handling, phone validation, SMTP health caching, SOCKS proxy loading, and a generic `web-command` dispatcher — is the shape of a bulk SMS/email abuse and proxy-routing framework, not a legitimate agent library. The `.ad/` directory prefix (hidden on Unix-like filesystems) plus uniform aggressive obfuscation across every file plus the mismatch between the package's generic 'agent' name and the shipped functionality all indicate deliberate concealment of the package's true purpose from installers. Tracing of the obfuscated code content was withheld by an automated malware-content filter — benign packages complete tracing normally; that trip is itself a corroborating signal that the deobfuscated code reads as operational abuse tooling. Installing this package places attacker-controlled obfuscated code into the installer's dependency tree that will execute when the package is loaded.",
